WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:24.270 --> 00:00:26.610
- Hi everyone, Sal Khan
here from Khan Academy.

00:00:26.610 --> 00:00:30.040
Welcome to our Daily Homeroom livestream.

00:00:30.040 --> 00:00:33.840
This is a thing we started
seems like a long time ago now,

00:00:33.840 --> 00:00:34.920
but it was several weeks ago

00:00:34.920 --> 00:00:36.560
when the school closures happened.

00:00:36.560 --> 00:00:39.610
Just a way to continue to
support you as a parent,

00:00:39.610 --> 00:00:41.500
a teacher, a student, obviously,

00:00:41.500 --> 00:00:43.340
Khan Academy has a lot
of resources like that.

00:00:43.340 --> 00:00:44.730
But there's something
especially in this time

00:00:44.730 --> 00:00:46.740
of social distancing, about being able

00:00:46.740 --> 00:00:48.460
to connect people live.

00:00:48.460 --> 00:00:51.150
And some days it's just me and it's here.

00:00:51.150 --> 00:00:52.430
I'm here to answer your questions.

00:00:52.430 --> 00:00:55.080
And some days we have
incredible guests like today.

00:00:55.080 --> 00:00:57.120
And so today, our guest is going to be

00:00:57.120 --> 00:00:58.100
General Stan McChrystal.

00:00:58.100 --> 00:00:59.790
I'm gonna be bringing him on a little bit,

00:00:59.790 --> 00:01:01.540
but I'll tell you all ahead of time,

00:01:01.540 --> 00:01:03.910
whether you're on Facebook or YouTube,

00:01:03.910 --> 00:01:05.970
start thinking of questions
that you would like

00:01:05.970 --> 00:01:07.550
to ask the general and put them in there.

00:01:07.550 --> 00:01:09.930
We have Khan Academy team
members who are looking

00:01:09.930 --> 00:01:12.070
at those questions and
will surface them to myself

00:01:12.070 --> 00:01:14.200
and General McChrystal.

00:01:14.200 --> 00:01:16.940
I do like to remind everyone somewhat,

00:01:16.940 --> 00:01:19.800
'cause it's important that
we are not for profit.

00:01:19.800 --> 00:01:22.050
We are funded by philanthropic donations

00:01:22.050 --> 00:01:23.500
from folks like yourself,

00:01:23.500 --> 00:01:25.460
we were running at a deficit even before

00:01:25.460 --> 00:01:26.800
this COVID crisis hit.

00:01:26.800 --> 00:01:28.420
And now with the school closures,

00:01:28.420 --> 00:01:30.850
we're seeing our traffic be almost three-X

00:01:30.850 --> 00:01:33.660
of what it typically is,
our registrations are,

00:01:33.660 --> 00:01:36.270
depending on the day six-to-20-X of what

00:01:36.270 --> 00:01:37.197
we would normally expect to be

00:01:37.197 --> 00:01:39.040
and we're trying to do more programs.

00:01:39.040 --> 00:01:40.180
So, if you're in a position to do so,

00:01:40.180 --> 00:01:41.570
please think about donating.

00:01:41.570 --> 00:01:43.270
I do wanna give a special shout out

00:01:43.270 --> 00:01:44.970
to several corporations who've stepped up

00:01:44.970 --> 00:01:49.210
in the last few weeks, Bank
of America, Google.org, AT&amp;T,

00:01:49.210 --> 00:01:53.680
Novartis and Fastly to
help us help you hopefully,

00:01:53.680 --> 00:01:55.290
with what's going on school closures,

00:01:55.290 --> 00:01:56.890
but we are still running at a deficit

00:01:56.890 --> 00:01:57.930
and we still need more help.

00:01:57.930 --> 00:01:59.580
So, whether you're an individual giver

00:01:59.580 --> 00:02:02.490
or a corporation or
represent a corporation,

00:02:02.490 --> 00:02:04.340
please, please think about that.

00:02:04.340 --> 00:02:07.650
So, with that I'd like to bring
on General Stan McChrystal.

00:02:07.650 --> 00:02:11.060
And General you're one of those people.

00:02:11.060 --> 00:02:14.874
You're a very decorated
person in your career.

00:02:14.874 --> 00:02:16.470
I could probably read your bio

00:02:16.470 --> 00:02:17.960
for about five minutes.

00:02:17.960 --> 00:02:21.870
But the headlines, you are
retired four-star General,

00:02:21.870 --> 00:02:25.990
you ran a essentially a significant
fraction of some of the,

00:02:25.990 --> 00:02:29.568
or all of the US military
operations and that we've been in,

00:02:29.568 --> 00:02:32.240
in recent modern history.

00:02:32.240 --> 00:02:35.380
And since then, you have
gone into the private sector,

00:02:35.380 --> 00:02:38.140
and you are focused on
helping advise folks

00:02:38.140 --> 00:02:41.630
on things like leadership
and organizational structure.

00:02:41.630 --> 00:02:43.810
Did I get that generally right?

00:02:43.810 --> 00:02:45.830
- Well, you probably gave me
more credit than I deserve.

00:02:45.830 --> 00:02:47.670
I was part of the team.

00:02:47.670 --> 00:02:49.523
But yeah, you're pretty accurate.

00:02:50.650 --> 00:02:52.450
- So, maybe a good place to start.

00:02:52.450 --> 00:02:53.900
You know, there's a lot of young people

00:02:53.900 --> 00:02:55.730
who are watching this and they're always

00:02:55.730 --> 00:02:56.920
thinking about their careers.

00:02:56.920 --> 00:02:58.680
They're thinking about
how do I navigate life?

00:02:58.680 --> 00:03:01.300
How do I build a sense of leadership,

00:03:01.300 --> 00:03:04.050
if you could just walk us
through your career arc,

00:03:04.050 --> 00:03:06.730
starting from when you were
maybe a high school student,

00:03:06.730 --> 00:03:08.560
but especially at once you
went into the military.

00:03:08.560 --> 00:03:11.310
And also a lot of us
don't fully understand

00:03:11.310 --> 00:03:12.780
how the military hierarchy works.

00:03:12.780 --> 00:03:15.500
And what are the expectations
as you become a lieutenant,

00:03:15.500 --> 00:03:18.160
as you become a one-star
general, two-star.

00:03:18.160 --> 00:03:19.600
How does that work?

00:03:19.600 --> 00:03:21.860
- Sure, yeah, I mean, you
only kind of figure it out

00:03:21.860 --> 00:03:22.990
in the rear-view mirror.

00:03:22.990 --> 00:03:24.300
I grew up in an army family.

00:03:24.300 --> 00:03:26.430
My father was a soldier.

00:03:26.430 --> 00:03:29.610
And his father was a
soldier and my four brothers

00:03:29.610 --> 00:03:32.460
all were in the army and my
sister married a soldier.

00:03:32.460 --> 00:03:35.070
Then when I married my
wife, she was the daughter

00:03:35.070 --> 00:03:37.507
of a career soldier and
her three brothers were so.

00:03:37.507 --> 00:03:41.250
So, you kind of get it,
we were the army business.

00:03:41.250 --> 00:03:43.070
From the earliest age I can remember,

00:03:43.070 --> 00:03:45.790
I wanted to be a soldier, like my father

00:03:45.790 --> 00:03:47.290
'cause my father was my hero.

00:03:47.290 --> 00:03:51.470
He was a very low key guy,
a decorated combat veteran,

00:03:51.470 --> 00:03:54.463
but very self effacing, very quiet.

00:03:55.865 --> 00:03:59.160
And so he was my idea of
the model of a leader.

00:03:59.160 --> 00:04:02.740
So, I wanted to do what he
did and go to West Point.

00:04:02.740 --> 00:04:05.650
So, at age 17, I went to West Point.

00:04:05.650 --> 00:04:06.950
Now, that was 1972.

00:04:06.950 --> 00:04:09.660
And to put it in context, the
military wasn't very popular

00:04:09.660 --> 00:04:13.100
in 1972, at the end of the Vietnam War.

00:04:13.100 --> 00:04:16.180
So, statistically speaking,
it was the easiest year

00:04:16.180 --> 00:04:19.020
in the academy's entire
history to be admitted.

00:04:19.020 --> 00:04:20.903
So, here I am.

00:04:20.903 --> 00:04:24.150
So, I got into the academy and I wanted

00:04:24.150 --> 00:04:26.430
to be an infantry officer.

00:04:26.430 --> 00:04:30.890
And West Point was 170
years old when I entered,

00:04:30.890 --> 00:04:33.530
and they took themselves very seriously.

00:04:33.530 --> 00:04:35.630
And I thought West Point
was gonna be sort of

00:04:35.630 --> 00:04:38.490
just a turnstile I go
through to get to my career,

00:04:38.490 --> 00:04:40.520
and I didn't take it very seriously.

00:04:40.520 --> 00:04:43.410
And so we had this clash of
cultures, me and West Point,

00:04:43.410 --> 00:04:46.460
17-year-old Stan and
170-year-old West Point,

00:04:46.460 --> 00:04:47.930
you can guess who won.

00:04:47.930 --> 00:04:50.380
And so, for four years, I
had kind of a rough go of it.

00:04:50.380 --> 00:04:53.480
First couple years, I did
horribly in discipline.

00:04:53.480 --> 00:04:58.240
I did horribly in academics,
almost flunked out.

00:04:58.240 --> 00:05:01.670
And then my last two years,
I started dating this girl,

00:05:01.670 --> 00:05:04.280
Annie, who I've now been
married to for 43 years,

00:05:04.280 --> 00:05:05.610
and she sort of set me straight

00:05:05.610 --> 00:05:08.920
and I started improving on those things.

00:05:08.920 --> 00:05:13.480
But you come out of West
Point basically schooled

00:05:13.480 --> 00:05:16.640
in things like character
and a college education,

00:05:16.640 --> 00:05:19.080
you're not a trained soldier.

00:05:19.080 --> 00:05:20.900
And so, the first thing you do

00:05:20.900 --> 00:05:22.420
is you go to some initial courses

00:05:22.420 --> 00:05:25.070
and your desire is to become technically

00:05:25.070 --> 00:05:26.970
and tactically competent.

00:05:26.970 --> 00:05:29.140
You wanna know weapons,
you wanna know tactics,

00:05:29.140 --> 00:05:31.000
you wanna be able to land navigate.

00:05:31.000 --> 00:05:34.800
So that when you go to
your first leadership job,

00:05:34.800 --> 00:05:36.610
as a platoon leader, you know,

00:05:36.610 --> 00:05:38.530
you'll be respected and
you'll be effective.

00:05:38.530 --> 00:05:41.160
So, I went through about
eight or nine months

00:05:41.160 --> 00:05:44.810
of additional training schools
to include Ranger School,

00:05:44.810 --> 00:05:46.380
where they sort of try to break you down.

00:05:46.380 --> 00:05:49.270
And then if you don't break,
you get this Ranger Tab

00:05:49.270 --> 00:05:50.440
and you go forward.

00:05:50.440 --> 00:05:52.470
But I got to my first unit, the 82nd,

00:05:52.470 --> 00:05:55.930
as a second lieutenant,
and it was '70s army.

00:05:55.930 --> 00:05:57.680
And it wasn't very good and it was still

00:05:57.680 --> 00:06:00.750
kind of Vietnam hangover and the troops,

00:06:00.750 --> 00:06:04.020
most of 'em came from
a difficult background.

00:06:04.020 --> 00:06:07.860
And so you walk in, a
college graduate, trained,

00:06:07.860 --> 00:06:10.620
and you think, okay, I'm
gonna lead these people.

00:06:10.620 --> 00:06:14.350
And then you realize that, in
fact, they know an awful lot.

00:06:14.350 --> 00:06:17.863
And although they would do some
amazing things on weekends,

00:06:19.240 --> 00:06:22.240
they actually take care of you.

00:06:22.240 --> 00:06:24.810
You take care of them,
but when it really gets

00:06:24.810 --> 00:06:26.670
to doing business, they take care of you.

00:06:26.670 --> 00:06:29.350
So from an early age,
you learn that the fight

00:06:29.350 --> 00:06:32.470
is gonna be won by the
sergeants and the privates,

00:06:32.470 --> 00:06:35.470
the lieutenant is going
to enable them to win it.

00:06:35.470 --> 00:06:37.820
And that gets more true
the further you go up.

00:06:37.820 --> 00:06:41.360
So, you spend the first, I spent
four years as a lieutenant,

00:06:41.360 --> 00:06:45.040
leading first about 30
soldiers as a platoon,

00:06:45.040 --> 00:06:47.120
as a paratrooper lieutenant.

00:06:47.120 --> 00:06:49.910
And then I went to Special
Forces and led a team

00:06:49.910 --> 00:06:53.113
of Special Forces non-commissioned
officers, Green Berets.

00:06:54.130 --> 00:06:55.310
Your next--

00:06:55.310 --> 00:06:56.220
- Just on that first appointment,

00:06:56.220 --> 00:06:59.140
I am curious, you know, you
were probably what, 23, 24,

00:06:59.140 --> 00:07:01.143
when you were leading that group?

00:07:01.143 --> 00:07:02.187
- 22, 23, yeah.

00:07:02.187 --> 00:07:04.020
- And I'm guessing most
of them were your age,

00:07:04.020 --> 00:07:06.130
maybe even a few of 'em
were older than you.

00:07:06.130 --> 00:07:07.190
How did you handle that?

00:07:07.190 --> 00:07:09.080
Like, did you ever feel
like an imposter syndrome?

00:07:09.080 --> 00:07:12.030
Or you know, how am I gonna
get the respect of these folks?

00:07:12.900 --> 00:07:14.240
- Well, it's very interesting,

00:07:14.240 --> 00:07:16.160
you do, the first thing is you wonder,

00:07:16.160 --> 00:07:17.590
are they gonna mind me?

00:07:17.590 --> 00:07:19.500
Are they going to respect me?

00:07:19.500 --> 00:07:22.210
But it was really kind
of a dangerous thing

00:07:22.210 --> 00:07:25.830
because what would happen was
the non-commissioned officers

00:07:25.830 --> 00:07:27.310
would be more experienced.

00:07:27.310 --> 00:07:30.520
And they'd have to be
sort of the hard asses,

00:07:30.520 --> 00:07:31.353
and the privates sometimes--

00:07:31.353 --> 00:07:32.580
- And what does that mean?

00:07:32.580 --> 00:07:34.760
What does non-commissioned officer mean?

00:07:34.760 --> 00:07:38.580
- Means sergeant, so you
have commissioned officers,

00:07:38.580 --> 00:07:40.630
which are lieutenants up through generals,

00:07:40.630 --> 00:07:43.180
and non-commissioned
means you are an officer

00:07:43.180 --> 00:07:45.470
but with no commission.

00:07:45.470 --> 00:07:48.770
So, it's sergeants, it's
from sergeant E-five

00:07:48.770 --> 00:07:50.520
up through sergeant major,

00:07:50.520 --> 00:07:53.220
and then below that are
privates and specialists.

00:07:53.220 --> 00:07:55.160
We call them our corporals.

00:07:55.160 --> 00:07:58.700
So, the military is
broken into enlisted ranks

00:07:58.700 --> 00:08:02.710
and officer ranks, and typically
people enter as a private

00:08:02.710 --> 00:08:04.260
or they enter as a lieutenant.

00:08:04.260 --> 00:08:06.340
Now, some people will enlist in it

00:08:06.340 --> 00:08:07.860
and become an officer,

00:08:07.860 --> 00:08:11.520
but the traditional way
is to come into those two.

00:08:11.520 --> 00:08:13.530
So, as you're a 22-year-old lieutenant,

00:08:13.530 --> 00:08:16.070
you've got sergeants
who are combat veterans,

00:08:16.070 --> 00:08:17.830
who have been around the block.

00:08:17.830 --> 00:08:22.170
And they have got to be
the biggest sticklers

00:08:22.170 --> 00:08:25.500
for discipline and
standards in the platoon.

00:08:25.500 --> 00:08:28.480
And yet you want to be in charge.

00:08:28.480 --> 00:08:30.660
So, it's this interesting dynamic,

00:08:30.660 --> 00:08:32.880
you have to earn their respect,

00:08:32.880 --> 00:08:35.690
but you're not really as qualified

00:08:35.690 --> 00:08:37.570
or as knowledgeable as they are.

00:08:37.570 --> 00:08:39.050
Good platoon sergeants,

00:08:39.050 --> 00:08:42.730
you have a platoon leader
and then a senior sergeant,

00:08:42.730 --> 00:08:45.000
sergeant first class, he's responsible

00:08:45.000 --> 00:08:47.980
or he feels he's responsible
for training you,

00:08:47.980 --> 00:08:49.540
for developing you.

00:08:49.540 --> 00:08:54.370
And if you get the right
relationship, it gets very close.

00:08:54.370 --> 00:08:57.840
And the lieutenant learns
to keep their mouth shut,

00:08:57.840 --> 00:09:00.760
learns to let the platoon
sergeant guide them,

00:09:00.760 --> 00:09:03.980
but the platoon sergeant
also learns increasingly

00:09:03.980 --> 00:09:07.460
to let that lieutenant take
more and more responsibility.

00:09:07.460 --> 00:09:09.900
Now, when you're young, you
can make some big mistakes.

00:09:09.900 --> 00:09:13.790
I made some very bad mistakes
as a second lieutenant.

00:09:13.790 --> 00:09:17.010
At one point I started
listening to the privates

00:09:17.010 --> 00:09:20.900
when they would complain
about the sergeants to me.

00:09:20.900 --> 00:09:23.160
I shouldn't have been
listening to 'em about that.

00:09:23.160 --> 00:09:25.230
But they would complain
about 'em and they'd go,

00:09:25.230 --> 00:09:27.230
you know, LT, those sergeants really

00:09:27.230 --> 00:09:28.920
are a pain in the ass, aren't they?

00:09:28.920 --> 00:09:32.660
And if you give them that ear,
and you start to think, wow,

00:09:32.660 --> 00:09:34.700
they like me, they don't
like the sergeants,

00:09:34.700 --> 00:09:36.830
so I'm actually a good leader,

00:09:36.830 --> 00:09:39.700
that's exactly the wrong conclusion.

00:09:39.700 --> 00:09:41.760
Because what really the reality was

00:09:41.760 --> 00:09:44.360
they're, you know, wily people

00:09:44.360 --> 00:09:47.360
and they are creating sort of a division.

00:09:47.360 --> 00:09:50.880
And it takes a little mat,
it took me some maturity

00:09:50.880 --> 00:09:53.070
to sort of grow through that era

00:09:53.070 --> 00:09:57.680
before I really learned how you lead.

00:09:57.680 --> 00:10:00.300
Another time, or we all have times

00:10:00.300 --> 00:10:02.030
when you learn about standards.

00:10:02.030 --> 00:10:03.550
Once we went to this position

00:10:03.550 --> 00:10:05.680
and I was pretty demanding

00:10:05.680 --> 00:10:07.860
'cause I wanted to have high standards.

00:10:07.860 --> 00:10:11.290
We went to this position and I
was leading a mortar platoon.

00:10:11.290 --> 00:10:13.620
And I made them dig the mortars in,

00:10:13.620 --> 00:10:15.350
which takes several
hours, three or four hours

00:10:15.350 --> 00:10:17.460
of really hard work for the whole platoon.

00:10:17.460 --> 00:10:19.850
And we dug 'em in, and then
we went to open the range

00:10:19.850 --> 00:10:22.630
with the range controller,
who allows you to live fire,

00:10:22.630 --> 00:10:25.080
and they told us you're
in the wrong position.

00:10:25.080 --> 00:10:27.620
And I said, wait a minute,
and it was my fault.

00:10:27.620 --> 00:10:30.090
I had read the papers wrong.

00:10:30.090 --> 00:10:32.430
So I had 'em digging
in the wrong position.

00:10:32.430 --> 00:10:33.830
I went to Range Control, I said,

00:10:33.830 --> 00:10:35.160
would you please let us change

00:10:35.160 --> 00:10:36.730
so that we don't have to move?

00:10:36.730 --> 00:10:37.970
And they said, nope.

00:10:37.970 --> 00:10:39.910
And so I had to go back to the platoon

00:10:39.910 --> 00:10:41.540
and say we have to move.

00:10:41.540 --> 00:10:43.000
It was dark, it was cold,

00:10:43.000 --> 00:10:44.270
and it was starting to rain.

00:10:44.270 --> 00:10:45.340
And I wasn't very popular.

00:10:45.340 --> 00:10:47.330
I said, fill the holes in, which they did.

00:10:47.330 --> 00:10:49.130
We went to the new location,

00:10:49.130 --> 00:10:51.350
and then you have this moral question.

00:10:51.350 --> 00:10:52.670
Am I gonna have 'em dig in

00:10:52.670 --> 00:10:55.210
'cause I said that's always the standard?

00:10:55.210 --> 00:10:56.780
But because of my screw up,

00:10:56.780 --> 00:10:58.910
they were gonna have
to do it a second time.

00:10:58.910 --> 00:10:59.840
And I remember that night,

00:10:59.840 --> 00:11:01.540
I said, okay, we're gonna dig in,

00:11:01.540 --> 00:11:02.780
we're gonna do it again.

00:11:02.780 --> 00:11:05.740
And there was muttering and
there was hate and discontent.

00:11:05.740 --> 00:11:07.460
And the only thing I can do was

00:11:07.460 --> 00:11:09.640
I got in and dug with 'em, of course.

00:11:09.640 --> 00:11:12.120
But you know, you just go through that

00:11:12.120 --> 00:11:14.650
and you learn that you're
gonna make mistakes,

00:11:14.650 --> 00:11:16.090
and you gotta own up to 'em.

00:11:16.090 --> 00:11:18.900
You gotta look people
in the eye and take 'em.

00:11:18.900 --> 00:11:22.087
- Yeah, I could dig into
each of these segments.

00:11:22.087 --> 00:11:23.027
(laughing)

00:11:23.027 --> 00:11:23.980
'Cause I think there's so much there.

00:11:23.980 --> 00:11:25.190
But sorry, I just, I,

00:11:25.190 --> 00:11:26.590
and we're starting to have
a lot of questions come in,

00:11:26.590 --> 00:11:28.620
but continue with that narrative.

00:11:28.620 --> 00:11:29.750
I mean, and some of this stuff

00:11:29.750 --> 00:11:30.910
you're saying very nonchalantly,

00:11:30.910 --> 00:11:32.950
like yeah, and then I started
leading some Special Forces,

00:11:32.950 --> 00:11:34.310
I mean, my understanding
of Special Forces,

00:11:34.310 --> 00:11:39.310
these are very hardcore types
of training and activities.

00:11:41.697 --> 00:11:45.240
- And you know, you do these
things to test yourself too,

00:11:45.240 --> 00:11:47.830
you wanna be in elite units
because you wanna see,

00:11:47.830 --> 00:11:49.200
can I do that?

00:11:49.200 --> 00:11:51.900
Plus, you wanna serve
with really good people.

00:11:51.900 --> 00:11:53.840
So, I went to Special Forces.

00:11:53.840 --> 00:11:57.260
And then I went to this
advanced course for a year

00:11:57.260 --> 00:12:00.210
where they really train you to
go from lieutenant to captain

00:12:00.210 --> 00:12:02.030
at about the four-year point,

00:12:02.030 --> 00:12:03.780
and then I went and as a captain,

00:12:03.780 --> 00:12:05.320
you're a company commander.

00:12:05.320 --> 00:12:09.420
Now, company commander
leads about 150 soldiers.

00:12:09.420 --> 00:12:12.450
And so at this level, you're leading

00:12:12.450 --> 00:12:15.010
essentially four platoons.

00:12:15.010 --> 00:12:17.710
So, you were a platoon
leader with 30, 40 people,

00:12:17.710 --> 00:12:19.997
now you're a company commander, about 150,

00:12:19.997 --> 00:12:21.890
and you have four platoons.

00:12:21.890 --> 00:12:26.280
Now you're sort of minor
level of executive leadership.

00:12:26.280 --> 00:12:29.460
But you are supposed to
be much more mature now.

00:12:29.460 --> 00:12:30.810
In fact, behind your back,

00:12:30.810 --> 00:12:33.190
they'll even call a company
commander at age 25,

00:12:33.190 --> 00:12:34.473
or six, the old man.

00:12:36.270 --> 00:12:40.260
Yeah, I was very centralized
as a company commander,

00:12:40.260 --> 00:12:43.360
my first company, I had a
mechanized infantry company

00:12:43.360 --> 00:12:45.260
of 14 armored vehicles.

00:12:45.260 --> 00:12:47.840
I put 'em all on the same radio frequency,

00:12:47.840 --> 00:12:49.930
so that I said turn
left, they all turn left

00:12:49.930 --> 00:12:51.940
at exactly the same time.

00:12:51.940 --> 00:12:54.220
And it was a small enough organization

00:12:54.220 --> 00:12:56.430
where I could micromanage it,

00:12:56.430 --> 00:13:00.560
you know, kind of put my
fingerprints on everything.

00:13:00.560 --> 00:13:03.300
And then I moved to
another company command,

00:13:03.300 --> 00:13:04.760
I went to the Ranger Regiment

00:13:04.760 --> 00:13:07.500
where it's a more elite
light infantry force.

00:13:07.500 --> 00:13:12.470
And the non, the sergeant's
there are very, very capable.

00:13:12.470 --> 00:13:14.100
When I got there in 1985,

00:13:14.100 --> 00:13:16.610
they were very good at their jobs.

00:13:16.610 --> 00:13:19.160
And they wouldn't
tolerate micromanagement.

00:13:19.160 --> 00:13:21.530
And so I got in there, and
I was sort of hard charged

00:13:21.530 --> 00:13:23.220
and I said, okay, here's
what we're gonna do,

00:13:23.220 --> 00:13:24.660
and I started micromanaging.

00:13:24.660 --> 00:13:26.990
And I got this incredible pushback.

00:13:26.990 --> 00:13:29.780
I mean, I got in your
face kind of pushback,

00:13:29.780 --> 00:13:31.740
because they'd seen a lot
of company commanders,

00:13:31.740 --> 00:13:34.550
and they weren't particularly
impressed with me.

00:13:34.550 --> 00:13:36.480
And they said, we don't operate that way.

00:13:36.480 --> 00:13:38.220
We are competent to do this,

00:13:38.220 --> 00:13:41.270
step back and let us do our jobs.

00:13:41.270 --> 00:13:44.730
And you know, it sort of
started my journey of saying,

00:13:44.730 --> 00:13:49.380
you know, okay, and when
they forced me to do that,

00:13:49.380 --> 00:13:51.650
we performed far better.

00:13:51.650 --> 00:13:54.860
And so that became,
started an arc in my career

00:13:54.860 --> 00:13:56.960
where I started understanding

00:13:56.960 --> 00:13:59.830
that my role wasn't to do things.

00:13:59.830 --> 00:14:02.280
It wasn't even really to
make a lot of decisions,

00:14:02.280 --> 00:14:04.090
it was to create an environment

00:14:04.090 --> 00:14:07.180
when really competent
people could be really good

00:14:07.180 --> 00:14:08.013
at what they do.

00:14:08.930 --> 00:14:12.970
You're sort of, then you
do some years on the staff.

00:14:12.970 --> 00:14:15.220
I went to the first Gulf
War and things like that,

00:14:15.220 --> 00:14:18.580
but the next big level
is a battalion commander.

00:14:18.580 --> 00:14:23.580
And that's about 600 soldiers.

00:14:24.160 --> 00:14:25.480
You're a lieutenant colonel,

00:14:25.480 --> 00:14:28.380
you got about 17 years in service.

00:14:28.380 --> 00:14:32.340
And you've got a staff
of about 35 officers

00:14:32.340 --> 00:14:35.260
and then 600 soldiers, I had paratroopers

00:14:35.260 --> 00:14:37.820
in my first battalion.

00:14:37.820 --> 00:14:40.930
And now you kind of are the old man.

00:14:40.930 --> 00:14:44.860
Now, you are pretty darn experienced.

00:14:44.860 --> 00:14:48.020
You've got a staff to make things happen.

00:14:48.020 --> 00:14:51.320
And yet you're still close
enough to the 600 soldiers

00:14:51.320 --> 00:14:54.390
where you know most of 'em
by sight and name at least

00:14:54.390 --> 00:14:57.310
and you can lead by example.

00:14:57.310 --> 00:15:00.680
Most officers will tell you
that's one of their favorite

00:15:00.680 --> 00:15:03.700
experiences because it's
a big enough organization

00:15:03.700 --> 00:15:05.210
actually do a lot and you get to do

00:15:05.210 --> 00:15:07.240
a lot of professional development.

00:15:07.240 --> 00:15:09.270
But you're still young
enough to be vigorous

00:15:09.270 --> 00:15:14.270
and leading from the front
and deeply involved in it.

00:15:15.160 --> 00:15:18.450
Every rank after that,
you start to get further

00:15:18.450 --> 00:15:20.040
from the soldiers.

00:15:20.040 --> 00:15:24.540
When you command a brigade,
you get about 3000 soldiers.

00:15:24.540 --> 00:15:26.417
And you've got battalion
commanders between you and them,

00:15:26.417 --> 00:15:28.560
and you're still the commander.

00:15:28.560 --> 00:15:30.800
But the reality is, you're starting

00:15:30.800 --> 00:15:32.500
to be a greater distance,

00:15:32.500 --> 00:15:34.820
you're starting to be a resource provider,

00:15:34.820 --> 00:15:38.170
you're starting to be
someone who shapes policy.

00:15:38.170 --> 00:15:40.210
And then as a general officer,

00:15:40.210 --> 00:15:43.520
that's really what you do,
you lead by inspiration

00:15:43.520 --> 00:15:47.150
and guidance, but you are
shaping things for the people

00:15:47.150 --> 00:15:48.550
who actually get stuff done.

00:15:49.770 --> 00:15:51.200
- And what's the difference,
you know, people hear

00:15:51.200 --> 00:15:54.520
about one-star, two-star,
three-star, four-star general,

00:15:54.520 --> 00:15:56.020
is there a difference between like,

00:15:56.020 --> 00:15:58.780
what divides those lines?

00:15:58.780 --> 00:16:00.853
- Yeah, that's a really fair question.

00:16:01.690 --> 00:16:06.070
By law, the army usually
has 303 general officers,

00:16:06.070 --> 00:16:09.930
half of whom have to be
one-stars, brigadier general.

00:16:09.930 --> 00:16:12.230
So, there are the four
levels of general officer

00:16:12.230 --> 00:16:15.220
except in World War Two and they had five.

00:16:15.220 --> 00:16:17.870
One-star general, typically,

00:16:17.870 --> 00:16:21.500
you're about 23, 24 years in service,

00:16:21.500 --> 00:16:23.270
you've been very successful because

00:16:23.270 --> 00:16:25.120
it's a real eye of the needle.

00:16:25.120 --> 00:16:28.650
They select 40 people
to be generals each year

00:16:28.650 --> 00:16:32.250
out of about 2,600 candidates.

00:16:32.250 --> 00:16:36.700
So, only 40 people in the year
group that enter in any year,

00:16:36.700 --> 00:16:39.160
get up and ever make one-star.

00:16:39.160 --> 00:16:41.750
One-stars are operational.

00:16:41.750 --> 00:16:43.100
They serve on some staffs,

00:16:43.100 --> 00:16:45.540
they're assistant division commanders.

00:16:45.540 --> 00:16:50.270
Two-star commanders, major
generals, command divisions,

00:16:50.270 --> 00:16:53.520
they command that's about 15,000 people.

00:16:53.520 --> 00:16:56.380
They are operational commanders.

00:16:56.380 --> 00:16:58.580
And then although they
don't talk about it,

00:16:58.580 --> 00:17:00.230
there's really a big difference

00:17:00.230 --> 00:17:03.450
between two-star generals and three,

00:17:03.450 --> 00:17:06.930
because three-star generals
is a pretty small group,

00:17:06.930 --> 00:17:11.930
I think it's about 50 in the
army, and they run big things.

00:17:12.160 --> 00:17:15.560
They run corps of 40 and 50,000 people,

00:17:15.560 --> 00:17:20.560
they run agencies in the
government, they do huge jobs.

00:17:20.840 --> 00:17:23.500
And then above that, there's
this another big jump

00:17:23.500 --> 00:17:27.490
to four-stars, and there are
typically 12 to 14 four-stars.

00:17:27.490 --> 00:17:31.540
And four-stars run the army completely

00:17:31.540 --> 00:17:33.500
in the Defense Department.

00:17:33.500 --> 00:17:36.030
Four-stars have immense power.

00:17:36.030 --> 00:17:38.210
All of the general officers
have a lot of power,

00:17:38.210 --> 00:17:41.029
but it's kind of like the Richter scale,

00:17:41.029 --> 00:17:45.240
your relative influence goes
up much more dramatically

00:17:45.240 --> 00:17:47.640
at the three and four-star level.

00:17:47.640 --> 00:17:49.020
- Wow, no, I could ask more.

00:17:49.020 --> 00:17:50.220
I mean, I'm learning a lot just about

00:17:50.220 --> 00:17:51.240
how the military is structured.

00:17:51.240 --> 00:17:52.767
I've always been fascinated by this.

00:17:52.767 --> 00:17:54.430
But I wanna make sure I get some questions

00:17:54.430 --> 00:17:57.170
from from YouTube and from Facebook.

00:17:57.170 --> 00:18:00.020
There's a lot of people are
asking a lot of good questions.

00:18:00.020 --> 00:18:01.640
You know, so from Facebook, Jenny Evans,

00:18:01.640 --> 00:18:03.200
and this is kind of a good balance

00:18:03.200 --> 00:18:04.520
between your military world

00:18:04.520 --> 00:18:06.500
and what you're now doing
in the private sector.

00:18:06.500 --> 00:18:09.290
How do we respond to poor leadership?

00:18:09.290 --> 00:18:10.770
So, I guess this could
be a situation where,

00:18:10.770 --> 00:18:12.050
you know, maybe your leaders,

00:18:12.050 --> 00:18:14.030
how do you manage around that?

00:18:14.030 --> 00:18:17.110
Or maybe someone in your
organization isn't leading well?

00:18:17.110 --> 00:18:19.330
- Yeah, that's always one
of the toughest questions.

00:18:19.330 --> 00:18:20.670
If you're in an organization,

00:18:20.670 --> 00:18:21.810
say you're in the middle of it,

00:18:21.810 --> 00:18:24.410
and the leadership above
you is not effective.

00:18:24.410 --> 00:18:25.500
It doesn't motivate you

00:18:25.500 --> 00:18:27.680
or it doesn't get the task done well.

00:18:27.680 --> 00:18:31.070
It's really hard to march
into your boss's office

00:18:31.070 --> 00:18:34.110
and say, ma'am, or sir,
you're not doing a good job.

00:18:34.110 --> 00:18:35.750
You need to do X, X and X.

00:18:35.750 --> 00:18:37.350
It's easy to say you do that.

00:18:37.350 --> 00:18:40.210
But most of us don't receive
that kind of visitor very well,

00:18:40.210 --> 00:18:41.440
for a leader.

00:18:41.440 --> 00:18:43.430
What I think we do is several things.

00:18:43.430 --> 00:18:48.430
First is we personify being
a good follower first,

00:18:49.010 --> 00:18:51.140
because then leaders will appreciate you

00:18:51.140 --> 00:18:53.070
and they'll listen to you more.

00:18:53.070 --> 00:18:56.160
Second, reflect the leadership behaviors

00:18:56.160 --> 00:18:58.223
you want to see in your bosses.

00:18:59.110 --> 00:19:00.930
Don't try to make it a difference,

00:19:00.930 --> 00:19:04.880
don't in front of the
people in the organization,

00:19:04.880 --> 00:19:08.360
don't try to make yourself
look better than your boss.

00:19:08.360 --> 00:19:11.670
'Cause it's just, it's
disrespectful and not helpful.

00:19:11.670 --> 00:19:14.920
But then have the kind of
conversations that say,

00:19:14.920 --> 00:19:19.260
ma'am or sir, this is what
I think might work better

00:19:19.260 --> 00:19:20.840
as we approach something.

00:19:20.840 --> 00:19:25.080
But again, I would tell you
put yourself in their position.

00:19:25.080 --> 00:19:28.100
Don't be self-righteous,
leaders are under,

00:19:28.100 --> 00:19:29.410
often under a lot of pressure,

00:19:29.410 --> 00:19:31.230
maybe sometimes they don't see it.

00:19:31.230 --> 00:19:34.420
If you say it in a way
where they are receptive

00:19:34.420 --> 00:19:35.840
when you're communicating,

00:19:35.840 --> 00:19:39.433
I think you have a much
better chance then,

00:19:40.270 --> 00:19:42.510
you know, being more negative.

00:19:42.510 --> 00:19:43.343
- I like that a lot.

00:19:43.343 --> 00:19:44.900
Like, you don't wanna just immediately

00:19:44.900 --> 00:19:46.900
you know, create a
stereotype around someone,

00:19:46.900 --> 00:19:48.040
climb the ladder of inference,

00:19:48.040 --> 00:19:49.890
and then somehow become insubordinate

00:19:49.890 --> 00:19:53.010
or try to embarrass them,
that's just gonna make it worse.

00:19:53.010 --> 00:19:56.470
Try to empathize a bit,
but speak your mind

00:19:56.470 --> 00:19:58.200
in an appropriate way.

00:19:58.200 --> 00:20:00.810
So, a lot of questions about
raising money kids here

00:20:00.810 --> 00:20:01.643
are around leadership.

00:20:01.643 --> 00:20:03.860
From YouTube, Wahkeena M,

00:20:03.860 --> 00:20:06.070
and then Adnan Aziz is asking variations

00:20:06.070 --> 00:20:06.903
of the same question.

00:20:06.903 --> 00:20:08.510
What advice do you have to raise kids

00:20:08.510 --> 00:20:10.800
who think for themselves
and become leaders

00:20:10.800 --> 00:20:13.930
versus someone who will
only do what they are told?

00:20:13.930 --> 00:20:17.830
- Yeah, well, you certainly
want your children to think

00:20:17.830 --> 00:20:18.970
for themselves 'cause you're not

00:20:18.970 --> 00:20:20.880
gonna be there with 'em.

00:20:20.880 --> 00:20:23.110
I would say the first thing about leading,

00:20:23.110 --> 00:20:25.960
you know, raising kids
is a form of leadership

00:20:25.960 --> 00:20:28.330
is reflect the values that you want.

00:20:28.330 --> 00:20:29.680
I've told people this before,

00:20:29.680 --> 00:20:31.790
but I had this wonderful father

00:20:31.790 --> 00:20:34.270
and mother in my entire childhood

00:20:34.270 --> 00:20:38.830
I never saw either of 'em
do anything dishonest.

00:20:38.830 --> 00:20:41.450
I never saw 'em take a parking
place they couldn't have.

00:20:41.450 --> 00:20:43.950
I never saw 'em, you know,
take a wink and a nod

00:20:43.950 --> 00:20:45.817
and keep change when it was given back,

00:20:45.817 --> 00:20:47.967
too much of it was
given to 'em at a store.

00:20:49.190 --> 00:20:53.660
That just everything I saw
them do was be kind to people.

00:20:53.660 --> 00:20:56.140
Be respectful to people
and even to us as kids.

00:20:56.140 --> 00:21:00.380
So, you start by being
people that the kids admire

00:21:00.380 --> 00:21:02.450
and would like to be like.

00:21:02.450 --> 00:21:05.433
I think after that, when
you've got your kids,

00:21:06.610 --> 00:21:07.740
each kid is different.

00:21:07.740 --> 00:21:09.900
I mean, that's the thing
we have to understand.

00:21:09.900 --> 00:21:14.050
My son, he grew up and
he's very much his own guy.

00:21:14.050 --> 00:21:16.440
And I was a soldier and I was, you know,

00:21:16.440 --> 00:21:18.270
pretty focused and whatnot.

00:21:18.270 --> 00:21:20.960
And in high school, he
had shoulder-length hair.

00:21:20.960 --> 00:21:24.380
And you know, I never gave
him a hard time about it.

00:21:24.380 --> 00:21:25.380
I laughed with him.

00:21:25.380 --> 00:21:27.470
I said, sometimes I'm
keeping pictures of this

00:21:27.470 --> 00:21:28.950
and when you get older,

00:21:28.950 --> 00:21:29.960
you know, we're gonna have a good fun,

00:21:29.960 --> 00:21:32.480
and then he had a mohawk through college.

00:21:32.480 --> 00:21:35.130
He was seven years getting
a four-year degree.

00:21:35.130 --> 00:21:37.340
He had a mohawk of different colors.

00:21:37.340 --> 00:21:41.830
He was a guitarist, lead
guitarist in a punk rock band,

00:21:41.830 --> 00:21:43.780
terrible music.

00:21:43.780 --> 00:21:46.210
But they were pretty successful,
I mean, they made CDs

00:21:46.210 --> 00:21:49.730
and toured and all, but
what the way I did it was

00:21:49.730 --> 00:21:52.490
I maintained the
relationship the whole time.

00:21:52.490 --> 00:21:54.530
I never tried to say,
you should be a soldier.

00:21:54.530 --> 00:21:56.460
I never tried to say, I just said, great.

00:21:56.460 --> 00:21:57.360
You know, you're gonna do that.

00:21:57.360 --> 00:21:58.880
You know, I joked about his hair,

00:21:58.880 --> 00:22:03.680
but we just, I thought
maintaining the relationship

00:22:03.680 --> 00:22:06.250
was gonna be more important
than anything else.

00:22:06.250 --> 00:22:09.690
Now he works for the US
government in an Intel agency,

00:22:09.690 --> 00:22:10.523
you know, who knew?

00:22:10.523 --> 00:22:12.190
And he's a father of three daughters.

00:22:12.190 --> 00:22:15.253
So I say, life comes around to
pay you back for your youth.

00:22:17.340 --> 00:22:19.360
- Your son's youth sounds a
little bit like some of mine.

00:22:19.360 --> 00:22:20.810
I tried to grow shoulder-length hair,

00:22:20.810 --> 00:22:24.470
but my it turned more into
kind of a sphere, but anyway.

00:22:24.470 --> 00:22:27.000
(laughing)

00:22:27.000 --> 00:22:29.950
So, from YouTube, there's
a sixth grade teacher,

00:22:29.950 --> 00:22:33.210
Virginia Garrett asks, what
is the most difficult part

00:22:33.210 --> 00:22:34.820
of being a leader and what would you tell

00:22:34.820 --> 00:22:37.490
a high school child about being a leader?

00:22:37.490 --> 00:22:39.810
- Yeah, I got an easy answer for this.

00:22:39.810 --> 00:22:42.080
The most disciplined
part is self discipline.

00:22:42.080 --> 00:22:45.040
If all of us took a
piece of paper right now

00:22:45.040 --> 00:22:48.450
and wrote down those things
we think good leaders do,

00:22:48.450 --> 00:22:50.950
we'd end up with some
pretty similar lists.

00:22:50.950 --> 00:22:53.170
And then if we all looked
at that list and said,

00:22:53.170 --> 00:22:57.140
what do we in our lives actually do?

00:22:57.140 --> 00:22:59.690
Our lists, we'd probably have
to strike a few things off

00:22:59.690 --> 00:23:02.610
'cause leadership is
sometimes inconvenient.

00:23:02.610 --> 00:23:04.600
It's sometimes frightening.

00:23:04.600 --> 00:23:06.503
It's sometimes awkward.

00:23:07.780 --> 00:23:11.440
And so, the difference, in
my view, between good leaders

00:23:11.440 --> 00:23:15.640
and not good leaders
is almost never ability

00:23:15.640 --> 00:23:18.240
or charisma or celebrity.

00:23:18.240 --> 00:23:20.830
It's the self discipline to do

00:23:20.830 --> 00:23:22.940
what you know you ought to do.

00:23:22.940 --> 00:23:26.170
And because that's hard,

00:23:26.170 --> 00:23:30.240
because it's all the time I
used to, when I was a four-star,

00:23:30.240 --> 00:23:33.390
you go around, we had 150,000
troops under my command

00:23:33.390 --> 00:23:35.350
in Afghanistan, and I'd go around,

00:23:35.350 --> 00:23:38.050
and I'd interact with a bunch of soldiers

00:23:38.050 --> 00:23:39.970
that I would only see
once, they'd only meet

00:23:39.970 --> 00:23:42.870
the four-star one time,
probably in their lives.

00:23:42.870 --> 00:23:45.420
And I'd have these interactions.

00:23:45.420 --> 00:23:48.360
And I would try very hard
to remember that however

00:23:48.360 --> 00:23:51.000
that interaction was,
they were gonna write

00:23:51.000 --> 00:23:54.810
or call home that night and
tell their spouse about it.

00:23:54.810 --> 00:23:56.120
If it was a good interaction,

00:23:56.120 --> 00:23:57.580
if I treated 'em respectfully,

00:23:57.580 --> 00:23:58.900
and you know, that sort of thing.

00:23:58.900 --> 00:23:59.830
They'd write and say, hey,

00:23:59.830 --> 00:24:01.800
I met General McChrystal, it was great.

00:24:01.800 --> 00:24:05.010
If I act a fool, they're
gonna call or write home

00:24:05.010 --> 00:24:05.910
and they're not gonna say that,

00:24:05.910 --> 00:24:07.100
they're gonna say something else.

00:24:07.100 --> 00:24:09.100
And it's not my reputation I think about,

00:24:09.100 --> 00:24:13.550
it's the reality that, that
interaction is important enough

00:24:13.550 --> 00:24:15.710
for them to call home about.

00:24:15.710 --> 00:24:18.640
It's important enough for
me to be disciplined with

00:24:18.640 --> 00:24:21.710
so that I treat them the
way they ought to be.

00:24:21.710 --> 00:24:23.820
Now, I didn't always get
it right, you get tired,

00:24:23.820 --> 00:24:26.280
you get frustrated, you get angry,

00:24:26.280 --> 00:24:29.110
and I interact with somebody
and as soon as it was over,

00:24:29.110 --> 00:24:31.130
I'd know I blew that.

00:24:31.130 --> 00:24:32.440
And then at the end of the night,

00:24:32.440 --> 00:24:34.830
you go and you're you're going to bed

00:24:34.830 --> 00:24:36.500
and you carry this sense of guilt.

00:24:36.500 --> 00:24:39.793
You say, I wasn't the leader
I should have been today.

00:24:40.710 --> 00:24:42.860
But you don't say, okay,
that's the new normal.

00:24:42.860 --> 00:24:44.310
I'm just gonna lapse into that,

00:24:44.310 --> 00:24:47.250
what you do say I'm gonna
try to do better tomorrow.

00:24:47.250 --> 00:24:51.040
And that is, the best
leaders I've known are never

00:24:51.040 --> 00:24:53.780
so self-confident in their leadership

00:24:53.780 --> 00:24:55.200
that they just kind of go around

00:24:55.200 --> 00:24:57.880
and let everybody bask in it.

00:24:57.880 --> 00:24:59.027
They agonize over it

00:24:59.027 --> 00:25:00.477
and I think that's important.

00:25:01.640 --> 00:25:03.710
- Yeah, I mean, what I get
from that is two things:

00:25:03.710 --> 00:25:05.300
Discipline, being able to do the things

00:25:05.300 --> 00:25:06.870
that you intellectually know are right,

00:25:06.870 --> 00:25:09.130
even if you don't want to do them

00:25:09.130 --> 00:25:10.300
or if they're uncomfortable.

00:25:10.300 --> 00:25:12.330
And then the second thing
is to just be constantly

00:25:12.330 --> 00:25:16.288
self-reflective and not
rigid in who you are.

00:25:16.288 --> 00:25:18.240
I guess you let your ego drive things

00:25:18.240 --> 00:25:21.810
is what I'm hearing a theme
throughout all of this.

00:25:21.810 --> 00:25:23.830
This is a question about leadership

00:25:23.830 --> 00:25:28.760
from actually from Poland,
Rafael Molechoradisky asked,

00:25:28.760 --> 00:25:30.910
what is the right balance
between challenging people

00:25:30.910 --> 00:25:32.620
and caring about them?

00:25:32.620 --> 00:25:34.240
And yeah, this would apply to leadership

00:25:34.240 --> 00:25:35.923
it might even apply to parenting.

00:25:37.020 --> 00:25:39.100
- Yeah, they used to say in the military,

00:25:39.100 --> 00:25:41.280
and I think it was Erwin
Rommel who first said,

00:25:41.280 --> 00:25:44.840
the best form of troop welfare
is first class training.

00:25:44.840 --> 00:25:47.100
And so what I would tell people is

00:25:47.100 --> 00:25:50.220
you don't have to be easy on people.

00:25:50.220 --> 00:25:52.710
You can be extraordinarily demanding.

00:25:52.710 --> 00:25:54.180
You can push people hard,

00:25:54.180 --> 00:25:56.820
you can make their life challenging,

00:25:56.820 --> 00:26:00.310
and that can be the best
thing you can do for 'em.

00:26:00.310 --> 00:26:02.910
Now, you don't have to be a jerk about it.

00:26:02.910 --> 00:26:04.690
You don't have to be ugly about it.

00:26:04.690 --> 00:26:07.650
But you can force people to do things

00:26:07.650 --> 00:26:10.020
they didn't think they could do.

00:26:10.020 --> 00:26:13.810
In fact, the leaders I
remember most fondly,

00:26:13.810 --> 00:26:15.790
that I owe the most to,

00:26:15.790 --> 00:26:17.980
were people who were
actually pretty hard on me,

00:26:17.980 --> 00:26:21.630
I always felt like I had
to be really working hard

00:26:21.630 --> 00:26:23.680
to meet their expectations.

00:26:23.680 --> 00:26:25.480
But most of 'em also had a personality

00:26:25.480 --> 00:26:26.940
where it was desperately important

00:26:26.940 --> 00:26:28.340
for me to meet their expectations,

00:26:28.340 --> 00:26:31.650
meaning I respected them
enough that their approval

00:26:31.650 --> 00:26:33.570
was something that mattered to me.

00:26:33.570 --> 00:26:35.450
But I would never shy away,

00:26:35.450 --> 00:26:37.720
sometimes we think we gotta be popular.

00:26:37.720 --> 00:26:40.290
Sometimes we think we've
gotta be rubbing 'em

00:26:40.290 --> 00:26:42.640
on the belly, you know, people
on the belly all the time

00:26:42.640 --> 00:26:45.250
and saying don't work too hard, etc.?

00:26:45.250 --> 00:26:47.950
No, we gotta get people
ready for the real world.

00:26:47.950 --> 00:26:50.090
And the real world is challenging.

00:26:50.090 --> 00:26:53.490
And so, in the real world,
they're gonna have to do it.

00:26:53.490 --> 00:26:55.223
So I think we owe them that.

00:26:56.740 --> 00:26:59.220
- Right, you can be hard on
someone as long as they know

00:26:59.220 --> 00:27:00.070
in the heart of hearts,

00:27:00.070 --> 00:27:02.290
that you're doing it
for what's best for them

00:27:02.290 --> 00:27:03.210
versus your own ego

00:27:03.210 --> 00:27:06.395
or you're just trying
to not be good about it.

00:27:06.395 --> 00:27:07.960
So a lot.

00:27:07.960 --> 00:27:09.000
I mean, there's a ton of questions.

00:27:09.000 --> 00:27:10.370
This is great.

00:27:10.370 --> 00:27:12.280
There, you know, this is interesting.

00:27:12.280 --> 00:27:13.940
Beth Otto from Facebook asks,

00:27:13.940 --> 00:27:15.880
what was your biggest
challenge as a leader

00:27:15.880 --> 00:27:18.463
in your career and how
did you overcome it?

00:27:21.750 --> 00:27:23.090
- Yeah, that's an interesting question.

00:27:23.090 --> 00:27:25.033
I had a lot of challenges.

00:27:26.160 --> 00:27:28.320
I think the first was being a micromanager

00:27:28.320 --> 00:27:31.180
and I kind of got people
beat that out of me.

00:27:31.180 --> 00:27:32.510
And that was good.

00:27:32.510 --> 00:27:36.170
Another thing is, I am by
nature very, very introverted.

00:27:36.170 --> 00:27:41.170
And so because of that, I
think if I die and go to hell,

00:27:44.470 --> 00:27:46.170
it'll be an endless cocktail party

00:27:46.170 --> 00:27:47.920
where you gotta make small talk.

00:27:47.920 --> 00:27:52.920
So, for me, it is being
comfortable engaging with people,

00:27:53.620 --> 00:27:56.470
not, I'm usually better engaging
with people I work with,

00:27:56.470 --> 00:27:58.970
soldiers and all, because I know my role,

00:27:58.970 --> 00:28:00.621
but it was something I had to work at.

00:28:00.621 --> 00:28:04.320
I'm not naturally
gregarious and back slapping

00:28:04.320 --> 00:28:06.690
and hanging around a lot of people.

00:28:06.690 --> 00:28:10.220
So, that was really important to me.

00:28:10.220 --> 00:28:14.680
And then I also find
that when I get tired,

00:28:14.680 --> 00:28:15.843
I get short tempered.

00:28:16.860 --> 00:28:19.300
And it's really, it's more impatient.

00:28:19.300 --> 00:28:21.570
I never yell or do that kind of thing.

00:28:21.570 --> 00:28:23.950
But what I do is I just get curt.

00:28:23.950 --> 00:28:28.260
And if I get pushed
hard, and I get that way,

00:28:28.260 --> 00:28:30.350
I'll start being very short with people,

00:28:30.350 --> 00:28:32.510
most of whom don't deserve it.

00:28:32.510 --> 00:28:34.630
You know, I've been lucky
that people work around me

00:28:34.630 --> 00:28:36.320
remarkably tolerable about it,

00:28:36.320 --> 00:28:38.083
because they'll know at
a certain point, yeah,

00:28:38.083 --> 00:28:39.970
he's getting tired, all right.

00:28:39.970 --> 00:28:42.970
He's starting to be the bad stand.

00:28:42.970 --> 00:28:46.439
But but it's important for me to

00:28:46.439 --> 00:28:48.980
constantly remind myself about that.

00:28:50.054 --> 00:28:51.740
- But were there times, just
following off on that question,

00:28:51.740 --> 00:28:52.960
especially since you talked about it

00:28:52.960 --> 00:28:54.700
when you were in kind of the
earlier ranks in the military.

00:28:54.700 --> 00:28:57.720
But once you were, you
know, a four-star general

00:28:57.720 --> 00:29:01.530
and you were deciding on things

00:29:01.530 --> 00:29:04.030
that could affect thousands of people's

00:29:04.030 --> 00:29:05.870
or even geopolitics broadly,

00:29:05.870 --> 00:29:07.920
were there times where
you questioned yourself,

00:29:07.920 --> 00:29:09.040
or you just really said,

00:29:09.040 --> 00:29:10.610
I don't know if I'm
making the right decision,

00:29:10.610 --> 00:29:14.000
or you kind of stayed up
at night and just said,

00:29:14.000 --> 00:29:18.000
the consequences are so huge
that it was paralyzing for you?

00:29:18.000 --> 00:29:20.260
- Yeah, most nights.

00:29:20.260 --> 00:29:22.460
You would make decisions
where you sent people

00:29:22.460 --> 00:29:25.090
into an operation where you know

00:29:25.090 --> 00:29:27.520
a number of them are gonna
get killed or wounded.

00:29:27.520 --> 00:29:30.590
And you just wonder if
you've done it right.

00:29:30.590 --> 00:29:34.110
Those are difficult.

00:29:34.110 --> 00:29:36.560
But sometimes others
are even more difficult

00:29:36.560 --> 00:29:38.120
when you've gotta fire somebody

00:29:38.120 --> 00:29:39.580
that you've known a long time.

00:29:39.580 --> 00:29:41.570
And they're a good person
and they're working hard,

00:29:41.570 --> 00:29:43.283
they're just not right for this.

00:29:44.460 --> 00:29:47.230
I would agonize over
those things tremendously.

00:29:47.230 --> 00:29:49.730
I remember, it was a much, you know,

00:29:49.730 --> 00:29:52.880
reported in the media when I
was a four-star in Afghanistan.

00:29:52.880 --> 00:29:54.600
I recommended to the president

00:29:54.600 --> 00:29:56.640
that we deploy additional troops,

00:29:56.640 --> 00:29:59.510
we expand our ability to operate

00:29:59.510 --> 00:30:02.090
and the president approved it.

00:30:02.090 --> 00:30:05.490
And I remember right after
we approved that, you know,

00:30:05.490 --> 00:30:08.960
I went into this reflective
mood and I said, okay,

00:30:08.960 --> 00:30:13.270
I've just asked for 40,000 more soldiers,

00:30:13.270 --> 00:30:16.603
a percentage of whom are gonna be killed.

00:30:19.100 --> 00:30:19.973
What did I do?

00:30:21.050 --> 00:30:23.460
And you know, I was able to
come back to this and say,

00:30:23.460 --> 00:30:25.730
well, I used my best judgment.

00:30:25.730 --> 00:30:26.800
I think it was right.

00:30:26.800 --> 00:30:28.800
And I do think it was right to this day.

00:30:29.910 --> 00:30:33.290
But I asked myself those kinds
of question all the time,

00:30:33.290 --> 00:30:36.740
would it have been easier
or better not to have?

00:30:36.740 --> 00:30:40.420
And it's not the heat or the public

00:30:40.420 --> 00:30:42.430
or media pressure that bothers you,

00:30:42.430 --> 00:30:45.540
it's the mirror pressure
when you look in the mirror.

00:30:45.540 --> 00:30:47.720
Did I do the right thing

00:30:48.930 --> 00:30:52.450
that I felt was right,
was it my best judgment?

00:30:52.450 --> 00:30:55.030
Do I really believe that
I did the right thing

00:30:55.030 --> 00:30:56.700
for the right reason?

00:30:56.700 --> 00:30:59.040
- And how do you, that's a
fascinating tension for me,

00:30:59.040 --> 00:31:01.420
you know, how do you
maintain that humanity

00:31:01.420 --> 00:31:03.740
where you're just like,
wow, these are 40,000 lives,

00:31:03.740 --> 00:31:05.320
you know, not only they're
gonna leave their family,

00:31:05.320 --> 00:31:09.190
but some of them might be
killed or injured in other ways,

00:31:09.190 --> 00:31:10.840
lifelong consequences.

00:31:10.840 --> 00:31:13.990
And so the empathetic side,
that could be almost paralyzing

00:31:13.990 --> 00:31:15.220
to just think about that idea.

00:31:15.220 --> 00:31:16.990
But then at the end of
day, you have a job,

00:31:16.990 --> 00:31:18.130
you have to be rational about it.

00:31:18.130 --> 00:31:20.593
You have to be kind of calculating.

00:31:22.340 --> 00:31:24.370
Do sometimes feel like you erred too much

00:31:24.370 --> 00:31:27.163
on one side of that or the other?

00:31:28.050 --> 00:31:31.740
- I think I always was
thinking about that,

00:31:31.740 --> 00:31:35.023
there were days when I thought
I erred one way or another.

00:31:35.950 --> 00:31:38.060
You know, Ulysses Grant was famous

00:31:38.060 --> 00:31:41.700
for pushing the the Union Army so hard

00:31:41.700 --> 00:31:43.610
and causing so many casualties,

00:31:43.610 --> 00:31:46.690
he would not visit field hospitals.

00:31:46.690 --> 00:31:48.760
Because when he went to the hospitals

00:31:48.760 --> 00:31:50.980
and he saw the tremendous carnage

00:31:50.980 --> 00:31:52.960
that he was responsible for,

00:31:52.960 --> 00:31:56.000
he thought it made him lose his resolve.

00:31:56.000 --> 00:31:58.680
And yet he had concluded that
the best thing he could do

00:31:58.680 --> 00:32:02.280
with the nation was lower his
head and just go on and win,

00:32:02.280 --> 00:32:05.100
even though the costs in
the near term would be high.

00:32:05.100 --> 00:32:09.850
I think he'd make those
balances all the time.

00:32:09.850 --> 00:32:11.780
Because particularly in the military,

00:32:11.780 --> 00:32:15.890
the force exists to
prosecute American policy.

00:32:15.890 --> 00:32:18.410
And if that requires us
to put it in harm's way,

00:32:18.410 --> 00:32:20.400
that's what it's for.

00:32:20.400 --> 00:32:25.400
You just have gotta balance
it against being cavalier

00:32:25.990 --> 00:32:29.130
or doing it for the wrong
reasons or not having done

00:32:29.130 --> 00:32:31.290
enough due diligence on it.

00:32:31.290 --> 00:32:32.853
There are a number of things

00:32:32.853 --> 00:32:34.620
that as a military professional,

00:32:34.620 --> 00:32:38.910
you better be asking yourself,
because people look to you.

00:32:38.910 --> 00:32:40.880
And they ask you, do we need to do that?

00:32:40.880 --> 00:32:43.330
And you, based on your experience,

00:32:43.330 --> 00:32:45.630
have enough respect
usually from that group

00:32:45.630 --> 00:32:47.120
that they listen.

00:32:47.120 --> 00:32:51.950
And so you need to be very
careful about what you suggest.

00:32:54.010 --> 00:32:55.800
- Yeah, no, this is fascinating.

00:32:55.800 --> 00:32:57.800
We're already out of time, we could go on,

00:32:57.800 --> 00:32:59.750
well, you know, hopefully we
can get you on this again.

00:32:59.750 --> 00:33:03.680
We have a lot of questions
for the message board.

00:33:03.680 --> 00:33:05.130
Actually, let's us
finish with one question

00:33:05.130 --> 00:33:07.650
which I think is relevant
to the time we're living in

00:33:07.650 --> 00:33:11.480
with you know, the social
distancing and COVID and all that.

00:33:11.480 --> 00:33:14.400
This is from YouTube, Brian 10 Eight asks,

00:33:14.400 --> 00:33:16.010
what advice can you offer for projecting

00:33:16.010 --> 00:33:18.560
calm controlled confidence in a crisis?

00:33:18.560 --> 00:33:21.020
Yeah, and a lot of times you
know that stuff's going on.

00:33:21.020 --> 00:33:24.520
But people are looking at
you to and your temperament

00:33:24.520 --> 00:33:26.210
to figure out what's going on.

00:33:26.210 --> 00:33:27.290
- In a firefight,

00:33:27.290 --> 00:33:29.920
as soon as it starts, the
privates look to the sergeants

00:33:29.920 --> 00:33:31.720
to see how they ought to act.

00:33:31.720 --> 00:33:33.420
If the privates haven't
been in one before,

00:33:33.420 --> 00:33:35.260
so they're looking to see
what are the sergeants doing?

00:33:35.260 --> 00:33:36.270
Should I be scared?

00:33:36.270 --> 00:33:37.570
What should I do?

00:33:37.570 --> 00:33:40.110
Even though they've been
trained in what to do?

00:33:40.110 --> 00:33:43.187
They're looking for example,
that's what leaders should do.

00:33:43.187 --> 00:33:47.740
So, I would say the
first thing is show calm.

00:33:47.740 --> 00:33:51.090
That doesn't mean you
should minimize the problem.

00:33:51.090 --> 00:33:52.470
You know, there's this tension that says,

00:33:52.470 --> 00:33:55.890
should I tell my people
how serious this really is?

00:33:55.890 --> 00:33:57.290
Am I just gonna scare 'em?

00:33:57.290 --> 00:34:00.740
Or should I project this overconfidence?

00:34:00.740 --> 00:34:02.910
No, I would be very candid with the team,

00:34:02.910 --> 00:34:05.510
say this is the situation.

00:34:05.510 --> 00:34:08.870
However, we are committed
to getting through this,

00:34:08.870 --> 00:34:11.070
we are committed to
succeeding in this effort,

00:34:11.070 --> 00:34:15.450
following through, and provide
inspiration, say there,

00:34:15.450 --> 00:34:16.380
we're gonna get through this.

00:34:16.380 --> 00:34:17.940
And after that, we're gonna go on

00:34:17.940 --> 00:34:20.290
to what we were trying to do.

00:34:20.290 --> 00:34:25.290
So, the leader provides this
candor, grounding in reality,

00:34:25.640 --> 00:34:29.900
provides this confident
commitment that says,

00:34:29.900 --> 00:34:33.340
like Franklin Roosevelt did in
his first inaugural address,

00:34:33.340 --> 00:34:35.610
he said, we're gonna
implement this new deal.

00:34:35.610 --> 00:34:37.090
We're gonna try a whole bunch of things

00:34:37.090 --> 00:34:38.950
and some of 'em won't work.

00:34:38.950 --> 00:34:42.370
We'll stop those, but we're
gonna figure out what works

00:34:42.370 --> 00:34:45.010
and go through it, and
suddenly provided hope

00:34:45.010 --> 00:34:47.040
to the American people.

00:34:47.040 --> 00:34:49.130
You don't ever wanna say
it's gonna be easier than

00:34:49.130 --> 00:34:51.960
it's gonna be because then
you lose your credibility

00:34:51.960 --> 00:34:53.130
when it's not.

00:34:53.130 --> 00:34:55.020
And so I think that leaders

00:34:55.020 --> 00:34:57.453
play an incredible role
during this period,

00:34:58.360 --> 00:35:01.060
even though we might not
have the right answer.

00:35:01.060 --> 00:35:02.320
You know, we might say, hey,

00:35:02.320 --> 00:35:04.490
I'm not sure what we ought to do.

00:35:04.490 --> 00:35:06.110
But I know we're gonna figure it out.

00:35:06.110 --> 00:35:07.730
And we're gonna stick with it

00:35:07.730 --> 00:35:09.703
'til we get to the place we need to be.

00:35:10.560 --> 00:35:14.103
- Yeah, I'm ready to follow
you, General McChrystal.

00:35:14.103 --> 00:35:16.219
(laughing)

00:35:16.219 --> 00:35:17.640
So, thank you so much.

00:35:17.640 --> 00:35:20.400
I think this was, I mean, I can
see from the message boards,

00:35:20.400 --> 00:35:23.330
I mean, this was really
useful for a lot of folks.

00:35:23.330 --> 00:35:26.030
And thank you for joining us.

00:35:26.030 --> 00:35:27.740
And hope we can do this
again sometime soon.

00:35:27.740 --> 00:35:29.070
- Well, you're kind to have me, Sal.

00:35:29.070 --> 00:35:31.280
And thanks for all you guys
do at the Khan Academy.

00:35:31.280 --> 00:35:32.870
It's amazing.

00:35:32.870 --> 00:35:34.370
- Right, thank you.

00:35:34.370 --> 00:35:35.203
- Thanks.

00:35:36.220 --> 00:35:37.530
- So thanks, everyone, for joining.

00:35:37.530 --> 00:35:39.550
Hopefully you found that
as interesting as I did,

00:35:39.550 --> 00:35:41.500
obviously, you know, it's
a shame that some of these

00:35:41.500 --> 00:35:43.350
only last half an hour, a little bit more,

00:35:43.350 --> 00:35:44.950
but we'll try to get more time

00:35:44.950 --> 00:35:46.330
with all of these folks that I think

00:35:46.330 --> 00:35:48.860
we can all learn a ton from.

00:35:48.860 --> 00:35:49.790
I'll just remind you,

00:35:49.790 --> 00:35:51.970
we're gonna be doing this every day.

00:35:51.970 --> 00:35:54.650
And so come on next week.

00:35:54.650 --> 00:35:56.810
We're gonna have a couple
of sessions with just me.

00:35:56.810 --> 00:35:59.700
I'm happy to answer any questions
y'all have about anything.

00:35:59.700 --> 00:36:00.770
And we're also going to have,

00:36:00.770 --> 00:36:02.270
we have a pretty fun lineup.

00:36:02.270 --> 00:36:03.220
We're getting a lot of momentum

00:36:03.220 --> 00:36:05.540
with really incredible
guests like we've had.

00:36:05.540 --> 00:36:06.860
Obviously, we had Ray Dalio yesterday,

00:36:06.860 --> 00:36:08.750
we have General Stan McChrystal today

00:36:08.750 --> 00:36:11.610
and next week we continue with this cast

00:36:11.610 --> 00:36:13.010
of really incredible folks that I think

00:36:13.010 --> 00:36:14.150
we can all learn from.

00:36:14.150 --> 00:36:16.470
I'll remind everyone again,
we are not for profit.

00:36:16.470 --> 00:36:18.510
If you're in a position to
do so, please think about

00:36:18.510 --> 00:36:20.090
donating to Khan Academy.

00:36:20.090 --> 00:36:21.960
It's those philanthropic
donations that allow us

00:36:21.960 --> 00:36:22.793
to do what we're doing.

00:36:22.793 --> 00:36:24.560
And we are running at a deficit right now,

00:36:24.560 --> 00:36:27.670
especially as we're trying
to support the world

00:36:27.670 --> 00:36:30.390
in our own way through the school closures

00:36:30.390 --> 00:36:31.980
that are going on right now.

00:36:31.980 --> 00:36:35.460
But with that, hope everyone
has a good and safe weekend

00:36:35.460 --> 00:36:37.083
and I will see y'all next week.

